On the "old" side off this diff I see it has an extra guard `rs.EachMode == NoEach`, but I don't see a corresponding guard in the new codepath. Did something else change in the meantime to make that unnecessary?

My memory of this (which, sadly, is rusty) is that the idea here was to preserve the resource object for a little while longer when `count` becomes zero (after formerly being >0) or `for_each` becomes `{}` (after formerly being non-empty), so that later references to the resource can still return an empty list or empty map correctly. We then have a separate graph node in the apply graph which aims to finally clean this up by calling `ms.RemoveResource`:

https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform/blob/6e909e3e8468599fb6eb32ff81fa8df750331e2a/terraform/graph_builder_apply.go#L108-L119

If I'm remembering the situation correctly, I think we could test it with a configuration something like this:

```hcl
variable "a_count" {
  type = number
}

resource "test" "a" {
  count = var.a_count
}

resource "test" "b" {
  foo = test.a.*.id
}
```

If we were to plan/apply the above initially with `a_count` set to `2` and then again with `a_count` set to `0` then hopefully we'd see `foo` inside `test.b` correctly set to an empty list on the second apply. If it were not working, I think it might turn up as an unknown value or some other error, depending on how the expression evaluation code handles not finding `test.a` in the state at all.

In particular, I'm worried about this part of the evaluator logic where it handles the resource state coming back as `nil`, which I think is what would happen if we remove it from the state too early:

https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform/blob/e32641c9ce8b7c1b766f7d0368a7aec93d3438ce/terraform/evaluate.go#L542-L552
